    "title": "Satsuma teen crowned Miss Alabama Teen USA prepares for nationals - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Kathryn Bruch ,\n6 hours ago\nSATSUMA, Ala. ( WKRG ) — A big moment for one Satsuma High School junior, as over the weekend she was crowned Miss Alabama Teen USA.\nAssault at Mobile McDonald’s leaves man injured “It was just so exciting, and when they called my name, I really couldn’t believe it,” said 16-year-old Britain Fuller. “And I knew to give all the glory to God.”\nPhoto courtesy: Michelle Watts Fuller is a junior at Satsuma High School and balances everything from classes, cheerleading, and multiple leadership roles.\nFour years ago, she got her start in pageants after meeting her now coach, Michelle Watts.\n“I went to my cousin’s school pageant. And Miss Michelle Watts was a judge there,” Fuller said. “Whenever I saw on her judge bio that she was a pageant coach as well, it lit a spark in me, and I knew that I wanted to be on that stage one day.”\nSince then, Fuller has spent hours at her coach’s house in West Mobile, practicing pageant walks, interview questions, and stage presence.\nPhoto courtesy: Michelle Watts “I used to walk with my head down in the halls, and I used to not be as confident,” Fuller said. “But she has really grown me to get out of my shell and walk with my head held high.”\nFor Fuller, the crown is about more than a title.\n“It really just means the world to me that I am able to do this,” she said. “Since I was a little girl, I’ve always watched pageants, and I have always looked up to them for role models. And now knowing that I get to be that role model is just so unbelievable.”\nAlong with preparing for nationals, Fuller has launched a program focused on keeping plastics out of waterways. She visits classrooms to read her own children’s book, ‘Don’t Eat That,’ and teaches students about protecting marine life.\nShe will compete for Miss Teen USA on Oct. 23 in Reno, Nevada.\nThree-vehicle crash on Airport Boulevard in Mobile leaves two injured Fuller has to raise $10,000 in just three weeks to cover expenses for the competition, and supporters can donate through her mother, Michelle Fuller’s, Facebook page or Venmo .\nCopyright 2025 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to WKRG News 5.\n4",
